Z||000001||Jim Jimenez||12-01-2001||02:34 AM||||You can repair your steering column, Why buy a replacement? The column has to be disassembled and the four anchor plate bolts need to be tightened. It requires some special tools like a lock plate remover and a tool to pull the pivot pins. Check the Fiero shop manual, they are well illustrated.If all else fails, find a competant repair shop that can do the job. Probably worth about 1.5 hours. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrossum||12-01-2001||09:33 AM||||if you decide not to fix it, i have a column for sale.........||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fierowrecker||12-01-2001||11:10 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jim Jimenez:<BR><B>You can repair your steering column, Why buy a replacement? The column has to be disassembled and the four anchor plate bolts need to be tightened. It requires some special tools like a lock plate remover and a tool to pull the pivot pins. Check the Fiero shop manual, they are well illustrated.If all else fails, find a competant repair shop that can do the job. Probably worth about 1.5 hours. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Jim is right!<BR>It can be done in the car or on the bench...<BR>Just be sure to clean the threads of the bolts and column and coat with locktite...<BR>Both mine and my wife's Fieros had loose stearing wheels, and now they are fine!<BR>crash...||||reg||1
